-- title:
--   Theorem 3.3 (corrected: weak convergence via survival functions) — the lattice $\mathcal L$ of constrained flows is Dedekind super complete; directed sets are reached by monotone sequences
-- statement:
--   Let $(S,\mathcal S,\pi)$ be a σ-finite measure space, $(L_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ Dedekind σ-complete sublattices of $(\mathcal M_{\le1},\le_{st})$ and $(B_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}\subset\mathcal S$. Let $\mathcal L$ be the set of measurable flows $\mu=(\mu_t)_{t\in S}$ of sub-probability measures with $\mu_t\in L_n$ for $\pi$-a.e. $t\in B_n$, for every $n$, ordered by $\mu\le\nu$ iff $\mu_t\le_{st}\nu_t$ for $\pi$-a.e. $t$. Then:
--
--   1. $\mathcal L$ is Dedekind super complete.
--   2. If $M\subseteq\mathcal L$ is nonempty, bounded above and directed upwards, then $\sup M$ exists in $\mathcal L$ and there is a sequence $\bar\mu^n\in M$ with $\bar\mu^n\le\bar\mu^{n+1}$ and
--   $$\bar\mu^n_t\longrightarrow(\sup M)_t\quad\text{weakly, for }\pi\text{-a.e. }t.$$
--   3. If $M\subseteq\mathcal L$ is nonempty, bounded below and directed downwards, then $\inf M$ exists in $\mathcal L$ and there is a sequence $\underline\mu^n\in M$ with $\underline\mu^n\ge\underline\mu^{n+1}$ and $\underline\mu^n_t\to(\inf M)_t$ weakly for $\pi$-a.e. $t$.
--
--   Weak convergence is pointwise convergence of survival functions at continuity points of the limit. The theorem is what makes the lattices of flows in §§5–7 complete and Dedekind super complete, as Theorem 2.6 requires.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Elements of $\mathcal L$ are measurable representatives with the $\pi$-a.e. preorder; the measurable structure on $\mathcal M_{\le1}$ is generated by the evaluations $\mu\mapsto\mu(A)$, which is the Borel σ-algebra of the weak topology. Directedness is `DirectedOn`; the convergence holds for every representative of $\sup M$ ($\inf M$).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: weak convergence of sub-probability measures (narrow convergence against bounded continuous functions) → WeakConv: pointwise convergence of survival functions at continuity points of the limit (the characterization stated on p.9); reason: with narrow convergence the downward part of b) is false: δ_{−n} is nonincreasing and bounded for ≤st (0 ≤st δ_{−n} ≤st δ_0), its infimum is the zero measure, and ∫1 dδ_{−n} = 1 does not tend to 0; the paper's proof uses exactly the survival-function convergence (DISPROOF.md)
--   - reading: Dedekind σ-complete sublattices (Lₙ) of M≤1 → IsSigmaSublattice: closed under ∨st, ∧st and under M≤1-suprema (infima) of nonempty countable subsets with an upper (lower) bound in Lₙ; reason: Example 3.2 b), p.10 ('a property that carries over to suprema and infima of countably many elements'); this reading admits every example listed there, including P(ℝ), which is not closed under infima of families bounded below only in M≤1
--   - encoding: L⁰_st: equivalence classes of 𝒮-B(M≤1)-measurable maps, B(M≤1) the Borel σ-algebra of the weak topology → measurable representatives S → M≤1 with the π-a.e. preorder; the σ-algebra on M≤1 generated by µ ↦ µ(A); reason: both σ-algebras are generated by µ ↦ µ((s, ∞)); IsLUB/IsGLB are invariant under a.e. equality
--   - encoding: bounded above or below and directed upwards or downwards … respectively; µ̄ⁿ → sup M ∈ 𝓛 → two clauses (bounded above + DirectedOn (≤) with a nondecreasing sequence; bounded below + DirectedOn (≥) with a nonincreasing one); the conclusion includes existence of sup M (inf M) in 𝓛; reason: 'respectively' pairs the alternatives; in a lattice x ∨ y ≤ z iff x ≤ z and y ≤ z
-- source:
--   Dianetti, Ferrari, Fischer, Nendel, A Unifying Framework for Submodular Mean Field Games, arXiv:2201.07850v1, §3, Theorem 3.3, p.10 (setting p.9, Example 3.2 p.10)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SubmodMFG_Supplement_SubProb
import Definitions.Def_SubmodMFG_Supplement_DedekindOrder
import Definitions.Def_SubmodMFG_Supplement_ConstrainedFlows

open MeasureTheory

namespace SubmodMFG.Supplement

/-- **Theorem 3.3** — the lattice `𝓛` of constrained flows of sub-probability measures is
Dedekind super complete, and bounded directed subsets are exhausted by monotone sequences that
converge weakly `π`-a.e.
Dianetti, Ferrari, Fischer, Nendel, *A Unifying Framework for Submodular Mean Field Games*,
arXiv:2201.07850v1, §3, Theorem 3.3, p.10: "a) The lattice 𝓛 is Dedekind super complete.
b) If M ⊂ 𝓛 is a nonempty set, which is bounded above or below and directed upwards or
downwards, then there exist sequences (µ̄ⁿ)ₙ∈ℕ ⊂ M and (µ̲ⁿ)ₙ∈ℕ ⊂ M with µ̄ⁿ ≤_{L⁰st} µ̄ⁿ⁺¹ and
µ̲ⁿ ≥_{L⁰st} µ̲ⁿ⁺¹ for all n ∈ ℕ and µ̄ⁿ → sup M ∈ 𝓛 and µ̲ⁿ → inf M ∈ 𝓛 weakly π-a.e. as
n → ∞, respectively."

**Formalization Note.** Standing assumptions of §3 (p.9): `(S, 𝒮, π)` σ-finite, `(Lₙ)` Dedekind
σ-complete sublattices of `M≤1` (`IsSigmaSublattice`), `(Bₙ) ⊂ 𝒮` measurable. `𝓛` is
`constrainedFlows π Ln Bn` with the order of `L⁰_st`; sup and inf are `IsLUB`/`IsGLB` in `𝓛`.
"Bounded above … directed upwards" goes with `µ̄ⁿ → sup M`, "bounded below … directed downwards"
with `µ̲ⁿ → inf M` ("respectively"); directedness is `DirectedOn (· ≤ ·)` resp. `(· ≥ ·)` (for a
lattice, `x ∨ y ≤ z` iff `x ≤ z` and `y ≤ z`). Weak convergence is `WeakConv`, and "π-a.e." is
`∀ᵐ t ∂π`, for every representative of the supremum (infimum). -/
theorem theorem_3_3 {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] (π : Measure S) [SigmaFinite π]
    (Ln : ℕ → Set SubProb) (hLn : ∀ n, IsSigmaSublattice (Ln n))
    (Bn : ℕ → Set S) (hBn : ∀ n, MeasurableSet (Bn n)) :
    DedekindSuperCompleteOrd (constrainedFlows π Ln Bn) ∧
    (∀ M : Set (constrainedFlows π Ln Bn), M.Nonempty → BddAbove M → DirectedOn (· ≤ ·) M →
      (∃ b, IsLUB M b) ∧
      ∃ μ : ℕ → constrainedFlows π Ln Bn, (∀ n, μ n ∈ M) ∧ (∀ n, μ n ≤ μ (n + 1)) ∧
        ∀ b, IsLUB M b → ∀ᵐ t ∂π, WeakConv (fun n => (μ n).1.1 t) (b.1.1 t)) ∧
    (∀ M : Set (constrainedFlows π Ln Bn), M.Nonempty → BddBelow M → DirectedOn (· ≥ ·) M →
      (∃ b, IsGLB M b) ∧
      ∃ μ : ℕ → constrainedFlows π Ln Bn, (∀ n, μ n ∈ M) ∧ (∀ n, μ (n + 1) ≤ μ n) ∧
        ∀ b, IsGLB M b → ∀ᵐ t ∂π, WeakConv (fun n => (μ n).1.1 t) (b.1.1 t)) := by sorry

end SubmodMFG.Supplement
